# SynthLink board font overrides — tier 0 of the font decision.
#
# A handful of boards are not drawn against CP437. An Amiga board's art is cut
# against Topaz and its high bytes are ISO-8859-1, so read as CP437 it comes out
# as a hail of guillemets and fractions. This file says which boards get which
# font, and the font carries everything else: a registry entry names the
# typeface, the encoding it is drawn against, and the column count it implies,
# so ONE id here settles all three. SyncTERM makes the same choice and says so
# in its manual — a font is chosen "and by implication, a codepage".
#
# The font named is the board's DEFAULT for the call, whatever the user last
# picked with the Aa button. It goes on once the call connects (the dial itself
# is drawn in the user's font), and while the call is up the Aa button opens a
# picker so a board that speaks more than one mode — 40 and 80 column PETSCII,
# or PETSCII and ANSI — can be switched by hand. A listed board always gets the
# picker, even when its default is the font already on screen, so a plain
# CP437 id is a useful entry too. Nothing is written to the user's stored font:
# hang up and their own choice is back.
#
# Format, one per line:
#   host:port  fontid     that board, that port
#   host       fontid     every port on that host
#   # comment             ignored, as are blank lines
#
# Hostnames are matched case-insensitively; a host:port line wins over a bare
# host line. An id no font answers to is ignored, with a line in the log — a
# typo leaves the board on the user's own font rather than on no font at all.
#
# Edits are live on the next /altfonts.json request (this file's mtime is
# checked), the same as config/curated.txt and config/blacklist.txt. Nothing to
# restart.
#
# The ids are in public/fonts/index.js. The CP437 ones worth naming here, for a
# board that also offers a PETSCII or other mode but should open in ANSI:
#
#   astpx8x19    Pixel — CP437, 80 columns (the default font)
#   vga9x14px    Squat — CP437, 40 columns
#
# and the board-specific ones:
#
#   topaz1200    Amiga Topaz 2+, ISO-8859-1, 80 columns
#   petscii40    Commodore PETSCII, 40 columns — and the PETSCII EMULATION with
#                it. A C64 board sends no ANSI at all: colour, reverse video,
#                cursor movement and the charset switch are single bytes, and
#                naming this font is what routes the stream to the parser that
#                reads them. One id, four things, for the reason above.
#   petscii80    Commodore PETSCII, 80 columns — SyncTERM's C128 80x25: the
#                same emulation with the CGA palette and the C128's 80-column
#                colour map.

# aBSiNTHE BBS is the board this was built for — CNet PRO on Amiga hardware.
# Its address is NOT filled in here because this checkout has never had it: it
# is not in curated.txt and nothing in the repo names it. Put the real
# host:port on the line below and it goes live on the next request.
#
# aBSiNTHE BBS
# <host>:<port>   topaz1200
absinthebbs.net:1940 topaz1200

# WORD BBS — Commodore-style board, 40-column PETSCII throughout. Confirmed
# against SyncTERM in Screen Mode C64, and tools/datasource/wordbbs-petscii.bin
# is a capture of a full session on it.
wordbbs.hopto.org:64128 petscii40
